>> title: Spinach Salad
>> category: Salads
>> tags: spinach, bacon, mushrooms, egg, warm dressing
>> counters: The Bowl Shop
>> aka: spinach salad, wilted spinach salad, warm spinach salad, spinach salad with hot bacon dressing, german spinach salad, bacon dressing salad
>> servings: 4
>> time: 35 min
>> slack: unforgiving — the dressing has about a minute at the right heat, and spinach hit twice goes from wilted to stewed
>> step.1: render 8 min
>> step.2: boil 7 min, chill hard
>> step.3: sear 5 min in the fat
>> step.4: whisk into the hot pan
>> step.5: peel and halve
>> step.6: wilt under the dressing

Render @thick-cut bacon{6%slices}(cut in 1/2-in lardons) in a cold #skillet{} over medium heat for ~render{8%min}. Every other salad here treats the fat as waste; this one is built on it, so do not pour it off.

Boil @eggs{2%large} for ~boil{7%min}, then ~chill{5%min} them in ice water. Seven minutes, not the cobb's eight — a jammy yolk breaking into a warm dressing is half of what this salad is.

Sear @cremini mushrooms{8%oz}(225 g; sliced thick) in @&(~2)bacon and its fat{} over high heat for ~sear{5%min} without stirring for the first two. Crowded and stirred, they boil in their own water and go grey; left alone in hot fat, they take colour and that colour ends up in the dressing.

Whisk @red wine vinegar{3%Tbs}(45 mL), @dijon mustard{1%tsp}, @light brown sugar{1%tsp} and @black pepper{1/2%tsp} into @&(~1)hot skillet{} off the heat, scraping the brown from the bottom. This is the dressing and there is no jar of it in the drawer, because it exists for about ninety seconds: it is bacon fat held together with vinegar and mustard, and it breaks as soon as it cools.

Peel and halve @&(~3)chilled eggs{}.

Wilt @baby spinach{8%cups}(200 g) and @red onion{1/4%small}(sliced paper thin) in a wide #bowl{} under @&(~2)hot dressing{}, tossing once, then set @&(~1)halved eggs{} on top.

Pour it over in one go and toss once — twice and it is cooked spinach. Half collapsed and half still raw.
